  "summary": "The Japanese art of folding uncut paper into cranes, frogs and geometric sculpture — geometry made by hand.",
  "description": "Origami matured in Edo Japan as both etiquette (formal folded wrappers, noshi) and play, standardized in the classic sequence: valley and mountain folds turn a single square into a crane, frog, boat or box without cuts or glue. Twentieth-century folders — Akira Yoshizawa above all — invented wet-folding, diagram language and thousands of models, turning a pastime into a mathematical art with applications from airbag design to space telescopes. The crane, folded a thousand times, remains its symbol of wish and peace.",
